[Term]
id: FBbt:00003654
name: adult ellipsoid body extrinsic ring neuron
def: "Ring neuron with extensive arborizations outside of the ellipsoid body (Hanesch et al., 1989; Omoto et al., 2018). Unlike the R-neurons, these neurons do not develop from neuroblast DALv2 (Omoto et al., 2018), and some of these cells are derived from each of BAmv1, DM6, DM4, and DM3 (Kandimalla et al., 2023). There are usually only one or two of each type of extrinsic ring neuron per hemisphere (Hulse et al., 2021)." [FlyBase:FBrf0049409, FlyBase:FBrf0240929, FlyBase:FBrf0254510, FlyBase:FBrf0257023]
subset: cur
synonym: "ExR-neuron" EXACT [FlyBase:FBrf0240929]
synonym: "extrinsic ring neuron" EXACT []
is_a: FBbt:00003649 ! adult ellipsoid body ring neuron

[Term]
id: FBbt:00003655
name: adult ellipsoid body extrinsic ring neuron ExR1
def: "Extrinsic ring neuron with a large soma located in the pars intercerebralis (Hanesch et al., 1989; Young and Armstrong, 2010). It follows the medial equatorial fascicle (Omoto et al., 2018) and arborizes in the dorsal fan-shaped body (FB), lateral accessory lobe (LAL) and bulb (Young and Armstrong, 2010; Omoto et al., 2018; Hulse et al., 2021). Its main projection then encircles the ellipsoid body (EB) and sends multiple evenly spaced projections that arborize in the inner central and anterior domains of the EB (Young and Armstrong, 2010; Omoto et al., 2018). Arborizations are mainly postsynaptic in the LAL (including the gall) and bulb, and mixed in the ellipsoid body and fan-shaped body (Hulse et al., 2021). There are two of these cells per hemisphere, one that develops from neuroblast DM3, and one that develops from DM4 (Kandimalla et al., 2023)." [FlyBase:FBrf0049409, FlyBase:FBrf0210154, FlyBase:FBrf0240929, FlyBase:FBrf0254510, FlyBase:FBrf0257023]
comment: Sites inferred to be presynaptic by localization of fluorescently tagged synaptobrevin and to be post-synaptic by localization of fluorescently tagged DSCAM (Young and Armstrong, 2010).
subset: cur
synonym: "helicon cell" RELATED [FlyBase:FBrf0240929]
is_a: FBbt:00003654 ! adult ellipsoid body extrinsic ring neuron
relationship: RO:0002100 FBbt:00003700 ! has soma location adult pars intercerebralis
relationship: RO:0002101 FBbt:00100342 ! fasciculates with adult medial equatorial fascicle
relationship: RO:0013002 FBbt:00003679 ! receives synaptic input in region fan-shaped body
relationship: RO:0013002 FBbt:00003681 ! receives synaptic input in region adult lateral accessory lobe
relationship: RO:0013002 FBbt:00003682 ! receives synaptic input in region bulb
relationship: RO:0013002 FBbt:00007553 ! receives synaptic input in region ellipsoid body inner central domain
relationship: RO:0013002 FBbt:00040060 ! receives synaptic input in region gall
relationship: RO:0013003 FBbt:00003679 ! sends synaptic output to region fan-shaped body
relationship: RO:0013003 FBbt:00007553 ! sends synaptic output to region ellipsoid body inner central domain

[Term]
id: FBbt:00003656
name: obsolete adult ellipsoid body extrinsic ring neuron ExR2
def: "Extrinsic ring neuron that is part of the dopaminergic PPM3 cluster (Omoto et al., 2018). From its cell body in the posterior cortex, it projects along the medial equatorial fascicle and innervates the bulb and the lateral surface of the lateral accessory lobe, bilaterally, as well as entering the ellipsoid body centripetally, arborizing in the outer center (sparse) and outer posterior (concentrated) domains (Omoto et al., 2018)." [FlyBase:FBrf0049409, FlyBase:FBrf0240929]
comment: ExR2 was not fully reconstructed by Hanesch et al. (1989), so although it is thought that they are represented by this class, it is possible that the Hanesch et al. (1989) ExR2 neurons are the Omoto et al. (2018) ExR4 neurons (Omoto et al., 2018).
synonym: "ExR2" EXACT [FlyBrain_NDB:10265]
synonym: "PPM3-EB" EXACT [FlyBase:FBrf0240929]
is_obsolete: true
replaced_by: FBbt:00110327

[Term]
id: FBbt:00003657
name: fan-shaped body tangential neuron
def: "Neuron with a large arborization field that fills a layer of the fan-shaped body (FB) in both the transverse and longitudinal directions (Hanesch et al., 1989; Hulse et al., 2021). Their arbors do not usually show a separation into columns (Hanesch et al., 1989). As a population, these neurons arborize in all FB layers, but most cells target one layer (Hanesch et al., 1989; Hulse et al., 2021). They generally receive input in other regions and have presynaptic sites in the FB, but some are intrinsic to the FB (Hulse et al., 2021)." [FlyBase:FBrf0049409, FlyBase:FBrf0254510]
