[Term]
id: FBbt:00052223
name: adult zorro subesophageal neuron
def: "Adult subesophageal local neuron with its soma in the ventral gnathal ganglion (Shiu et al., 2022). It receives gustatory input, mainly from sugar-sensing neurons and its activation results in proboscis extension (Shiu et al., 2022). It is bilateral, but most of its innervation is in the ipsilateral hemisphere (Shiu et al., 2022). It is predicted to be cholinergic (Shiu et al., 2022). There are 2 of these cells per organism (Dorkenwald et al., 2024; Schlegel et al., 2024)." [FlyBase:FBrf0254006, FlyBase:FBrf0260535, FlyBase:FBrf0260546]
comment: Neurotransmitter prediction based on machine learning classification (Shiu et al., 2022). Named based on 'Z' morphology of proximal neurite (Shiu et al., 2022). FlyWire CB0192 mapped to zorro in Shiu et al. (2024) - FBrf0260565 supplement.
synonym: "adult CB0192 neuron" EXACT [FlyBase:FBrf0260565]
is_a: FBbt:00051493 ! local neuron
relationship: RO:0000053 PATO:0000618 ! has characteristic bilateral
relationship: RO:0002100 FBbt:00100210 ! has soma location cell body rind of adult gnathal ganglion
relationship: RO:0002215 GO:0014055 ! capable of acetylcholine secretion, neurotransmission
relationship: RO:0002216 GO:0008343 ! capable of part of adult feeding behavior
relationship: RO:0013002 FBbt:00014013 ! receives synaptic input in region adult gnathal ganglion
relationship: RO:0013003 FBbt:00014013 ! sends synaptic output to region adult gnathal ganglion
relationship: RO:0013008 FBbt:00049614 ! receives synaptic input from neuron sugar-sensing neuron
property_value: http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1373-1705
property_value: http://purl.org/dc/terms/date "2022-07-14T14:42:49Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: FBbt:00052224
name: adult scapula subesophageal neuron
def: "Adult interneuron that receives a large amount of input from bitter-sensing gustatory neurons in the subesophageal zone and sends inhibitory (predicted glutamatergic) input to feeding premotor neurons (Shiu et al., 2022)." [FlyBase:FBrf0254006]
comment: Neurotransmitter prediction based on machine learning classification (Shiu et al., 2022). Named based on resemblance to an inverted scapula bone and proximity to clavicle neuron (Shiu et al., 2022).
